   "summary": "Coal-company clerk and supervisor in Wilkes-Barre (about 1904–1963), born in Tamaqua, son of Henry J. Meier and Anna Higgins and husband of Florence Pringle; John's great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "William F. Meier was born about 1904 in Tamaqua, Schuylkill County, Pennsylvania, where his family was living in 1905 and 1906; the records disagree on the exact year, placing it between 1903 and 1905. He was the son of Henry J. Meier, a German-born iron moulder, and Anna Higgins. His brothers and sisters were Fred Aloysius (1892–1966), Henry James Jr. (1894–1957), Mary C. (1897–1962), Madeline Edelberta (1900–1995) and Joseph John (1906–1970); one more child had died by 1910. By 1910 the family had moved to Mill Street in Wilkes-Barre, and in 1920, at 15, William was an errand boy at a drug store while living with his parents on Barney Street.\n\nOn 20 April 1926, in Wilkes-Barre, he married Florence Louise Pringle, a clerk born in Plymouth and the daughter of the carpenter Addison Pringle and Catherine Heffernan; William was then a clerk of 22 living on Barney Street, and the Rev. D. W. McCarthy performed the ceremony. By 1930 he was a clerk in a coal office, living on Cedar Street with Florence and their sons William and James, close to the Pringle family on Huston Street. In 1950 he was a supervisor at a coal company, still on Cedar Street, with Florence, William Jr., James C. and Barbara Ann. His father Henry lived on until December 1950, dying at 83 on Barney Street, when William was 46. The Florence Irene Meier buried from Wilkes-Barre in October 1933 was not his daughter but his niece, the 17-year-old eldest child of his brother Fred Aloysius Meier and Anna Mather.\n\nWilliam died in Wilkes-Barre on 4 July 1963, aged 59. He is John's great-grandfather and the father of James C. Meier.\n\nThe family name was spelt Mayer in the Munzingen registers of Baden from 1820 to 1862, Meyer in the 1870 United States census and Myer in 1880; the first record to write it Meier was the 1892 marriage record of his father Henry J. Meier, written by the clerk, so Henry may not have chosen the spelling himself. From 1910 on it was consistently Meier, apart from a Meyer in the 1940 census, and no formal change of name was ever recorded.",
