   "summary": "John's great-grandmother, daughter of Addison F. Pringle and Catherine E. Heffernan of Plymouth, whose marriage to William F. Meier joined the Meier line to the Pringle and Heffernan families.",
   "bio": "Florence Louise Pringle was born at Plymouth, Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, about 1903 or 1904; the records differ by a year. She was one of eight known children of Addison F. Pringle and Catherine E. (Heffernan) Pringle, her brothers and sisters being Genevieve (1893), Joseph Vincent (1895), Mary E. (1898), Anna Theresa (1901), Helen (1906), Francis (1909) and Catherine (1912). In 1910 the family was living at Larksville, where the McGuire family of her future daughter-in-law also lived.\n\nBy 1926 Florence was a clerk living with her parents on Huston Street in Wilkes-Barre. On 20 April 1926 she married William F. Meier, a clerk born at Tamaqua, at Wilkes-Barre; the ceremony was performed by Rev. D. W. McCarthy. In 1930 she and William were living on Cedar Street with their sons William, about three, and James, not yet two, close to her parents. Her father's obituary in 1943 listed her as 'Mrs. William Meier, Wilkes-Barre'. By 1950 the household consisted of William F., then a supervisor at a coal company, Florence, William Jr., James C. and Barbara Ann. An infant named Florence Irene Meier, whose funeral notice appeared in Wilkes-Barre in October 1933, may have been a daughter who died young, though that is not certain.\n\nWilliam died at Wilkes-Barre on 4 July 1963. Florence died at Wilkes-Barre in 1980. Through her son James C. Meier she was John's great-grandmother, and her marriage is the link that joins the Meier line to the Pringle, Heffernan, Lamoreaux and colonial Long Island lines.",
   "funFacts": [
    "In 1910 the Pringles and the McGuires (family of her future daughter-in-law Kathryn McGuire) both lived in small-town Larksville, 18 years before their children married.",
    "In 1930 she lived on the same census sheet as her parents: Meiers at 55 Cedar St, Pringles at 28 Huston St.",
    "Both she and her husband were clerks when they married: a white-collar generation, one step up from her uncle Orange Pringle, a slate picker at 13 in 1880."
   ],
