   "summary": "Plymouth Township laborer, son of James and Elizabeth (Croup) Pringle, whose wife Catherine Scott divorced him; John's 3rd-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Andrew G. Pringle was born about 1836, probably in Plymouth Township, Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, the son of James Pringle, a farmer, and Elizabeth Croup, and a grandson of Samuel and Martha (Lamoreaux) Pringle. The meaning of his middle initial is unknown. In 1850, aged 14, he was at home with his parents and with Alby, Noah, Leva Ann and Edwin; the widow Hulda Pringle and her children lived two doors away. His descent is the link through which his son Addison Pringle, and John's family after him, descends from the Croup and Lamoreaux lines, the route by which the family's Revolutionary ancestry is proved.\n\nIn 1857 he married Catherine Scott of Kingston. In 1860 he was a laborer of 24 living in Plymouth with Catharine, 23, and their daughter Ellen, 2, next door to his parents. By 1870 he owned $500 in real estate, and the household had grown to Ellen Jane, 12, John, 8, Samuel, 7, Addison, 5, Orange, 3, and a four-month-old girl, Julia, probably named Julia Ann after Catherine's mother; his father James, 71, widowed or separated, was living with them. In 1880 he was still a laborer, with Katharine, 43, John, 20, Samuel, 17, Addison, 15, also a laborer, Orange, 13, a slate picker, and Stella, 5; a 15-year-old servant next door, Emma Pringle, was a relative of unknown connection. In naming sons Orange and Samuel he followed the old Lamoreaux and Pringle pattern.\n\nThe marriage ended in divorce. Catherine brought the action, which the court sustained; the grounds are not known. By 1900 she described herself as divorced, and by 1910 as a widow, so Andrew had died by then; family records put his death in 1900 in Luzerne County. Their seven children were Ellen Jane, John, Samuel, Addison, Orange, Julia and Stella, five of whom were living in 1900.",
