   "summary": "Nurse of Plymouth who won a divorce from Andrew Pringle and raised seven children, praised in 1893 as bearing \"a name beyond reproach\"; John's 3rd-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Catherine Scott was born at Kingston, Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, on 14 May 1837 (some records give 2 May 1838), the third of the six living children of Stephen and Julia Ann Scott. She was fourteen when the 1850 census found the family at Kingston. In 1857 she married Andrew Pringle, and in 1860 the young couple were living in Plymouth next to his parents. They had seven children: Ellen Jane, John, Samuel, Addison, Orange, a daughter Julia who was an infant in 1870, and Stella.\n\nThe marriage ended in divorce, in an action brought by Catherine herself and sustained by the court, after which she resumed her maiden name, though she remained better known as Pringle. By 1893 she was a working nurse in Plymouth, a member of the Christian Church, the owner of a house and lot, and had raised her children; the county historian Bradsby wrote that she \"has always had, and now bears, a name beyond reproach\" and \"carries on her business with marked ability\". He named her living children as John, who married Regina Stires, Ellen, who married Francis Randell, Samuel, Orange and Estella.\n\nIn 1900 she was living in Plymouth Township in the household of her son Orange, recorded as divorced and as the mother of seven children of whom five were living, with her son Addison's family next door and probably her daughter Ella Randall a house further on. By 1910 she was at Larksville, still with Orange, and described herself as a widow aged 71. That census was the first to say her parents had been born in Connecticut, as Bradsby had also stated in 1893, though earlier censuses had given Pennsylvania; where the Scotts came from remains an open question.\n\nShe died at Larksville on 3 September 1918, registered under her married name of Pringle, and was buried on 6 September in the Dallas area. Some records give 1910 as her death year, but 1918 is the better supported date. She was John's 3rd-great-grandmother.",
   "funFacts": [
    "A divorced woman who reverted to her maiden name in the 19th century - rare at the time.",
    "Bradsby's sketch is the only published biography of a direct Pringle-line woman."
   ],
