   "bio": "James Pringle was born about 1799 in Pennsylvania, a son of Samuel Pringle and Martha Lamoreaux. He married Elizabeth Croup, daughter of Frederick Croup and Mary Young of Hunlock's Creek and Nanticoke, and farmed in Plymouth Township, Luzerne County, where he was living by 1840. In 1850 his household, with real estate worth $500, was James, 51, Elizabeth, 48, and the children Alby, 27, Noah, 16, Andrew, 14, Leva Ann, 11, and Edwin, 9; the widow Hulda Pringle, probably his sister-in-law, lived nearby. He was not the runaway apprentice James Pringle, about fourteen, advertised by Isaac Smith of Exeter Township in April 1836, nor his uncle James Pringle who married Phebe Lamoreux in 1785.\n\nIn August 1860 James, 61, was still farming in Plymouth, with his real estate now valued at $1,200, more than double the figure of 1850. His wife Elizabeth, 57, and youngest son Edwin, 18, were at home, together with a seven-year-old, Mary Williams, perhaps a grandchild or a bound girl, and his son Andrew had set up house next door. By July 1870 James, 71, was living in Andrew's household and Elizabeth was no longer listed, so she had probably died during the 1860s. James himself died at Plymouth some time after July 1870.\n\nSome family records give him a second wife, Mary Steele, who died on 15 July 1876, and a burial in Scott Cemetery, Huntington Mills. But Mary Steele was the wife of John Linskill, who died in 1865, the mother of six Linskill children born in Lehman Township between 1833 and 1848, and was buried as Mary Steele Linskill at Huntsville, Jackson Township. She was James's first cousin, a daughter of Peter Steele and Charity Lamoreaux, which may explain the confusion; the supposed second marriage should be treated as an error unless a record proves it.",
