   "summary": "Daughter of the Palatine-German Croup family of Nanticoke and Hunlock's Creek (about 1802–1895), wife of James Pringle of Plymouth; John's 4th-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Elizabeth Croup, whose family name was also written Grub and Croop, was born about 1802, probably in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania. Her parents, Frederick Croup and Mary Young, had moved there from Forks Twp in Northampton County about 1799 or 1800, when her sister Susan was born at Nanticoke in May 1800; the Croups were a Palatine-German family settled at Hunlock's Creek and Nanticoke. Her brothers and sisters were Susan, John, Peter, Mary, Margaret, Catherine, Ann and William. Her father died in 1841, leaving a will as a resident of Plymouth Twp.\n\nShe married James Pringle and had at least five children with him, among them John's ancestor Andrew G. Pringle. In August 1860, aged 57, she was living on the family farm in Plymouth with James and their youngest son Edwin, with Andrew next door. By July 1870 James was living with Andrew and Elizabeth was no longer in the household. Family records say she died in 1895 in Indiana, which would fit her having gone west with relatives, as her niece Mary (Shupp) Nesbitt had done when she moved to Rolling Prairie, Indiana, in 1853; it is also possible that she died in the 1860s. She is John's 4th-great-grandmother.",
