   "summary": "John's 5th-great-grandfather, a Connecticut-born Pringle or Prindle who married Ensign Thomas Lamoreaux's daughter in Orange County, New York, and pioneered at Plymouth, Pennsylvania.",
   "bio": "Samuel Pringle, whose name was often written Prindle, was born about 1767, by most accounts in Connecticut. He and his brother James, 'presumably natives of Connecticut', lived several years in Orange County, New York, before 1790. The family was said to descend from a Scottish emigrant, and several Pringles and Prindles were among the original purchasers of New Milford, Connecticut, in 1703; Newtown and New Milford in Fairfield County are the probable home area. He may have been a son of James Prindle of Newtown (born 5 August 1736, married Anar or Hannah Turner on 22 January 1759, died at Newtown in 1800), but that reconstruction includes elder children born before the 1759 marriage and remains speculative. Benjamin Prindle, a private in the Orange County militia, looks like a brother rather than his father.\n\nIn Orange County the Prindle brothers and sisters married into the Lamoreaux family four times: David married Susannah Lamoreux in 1783, James married Phebe in 1785, Hannah married Isaac Lamoreux in 1790, and in 1790, at Smith's Clove, Samuel married Martha Lamoreaux, eldest daughter of Ensign Thomas and Keturah Lamoreaux. The Pringles and the Lamoreauxs then moved to Plymouth in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, the Pringles in 1794 by one account, though Thomas Lamoreaux himself may have gone as early as the spring of 1790.\n\nSamuel was on the Plymouth assessment list of 1796 and was Supervisor of Roads in 1800. The 1800 census lists 'Samuel Prindle' and 'James Prindle' one after the other among the Lamoreaux, Steel, Van Loon and Davenport households, Samuel's household being a man and a woman aged between 26 and 44 with four boys and a girl under ten. He built a stone farmhouse near what became the Gaylord coal shaft; by 1873 'heaps of culm and stacks of machinery' had so defaced the place that, a local writer thought, if the old farmer's spirit returned 'it is very doubtful whether he could recognize the locality'. The account books of an early Plymouth store list him among the regular customers buying 'rum, whisky, tobacco and snuff'.\n\nSamuel died on 25 March 1847 in Kingston Township and was buried in Shupp's Cemetery at Larksville. He and Martha had eight children, five of them sons. Those identified are Thomas (1791–1861), Benjamin (1794–1853), James (born about 1799), Temperance (born about 1802) and probably Samuel Jr., who married Huldah Ives; a John who married Phoebe Davenport may also have been theirs. Samuel was John's 5th-great-grandfather.",
   "funFacts": [
    "His stone farmhouse was swallowed by culm heaps near the Gaylord shaft by 1873.",
    "The store ledger shows his household's luxuries: rum, whisky, tobacco and snuff."
   ],
