   "summary": "Southold-born Tuthill who farmed at Brookhaven and moved to the Orange County highlands in the 1740s; John's 8th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "James Tuthill was born about 1692 at Oysterponds, now Orient, in Southold, Suffolk County, New York, the son of John Tuthill Jr. and Mehitable Wells. About 1714 he married Rachel Browne. Some family records give his wife as Hannah Reeve (1699–1764), daughter of Maj. James Reeve of Mattituck, but Hannah was in fact the wife of his brother Joshua Tuthill of Cutchogue, and her gravestone belongs to that marriage; because James and Joshua were full brothers, the Tuthill, King and Wells ancestry stands either way.\n\nJames bought land at Brookhaven in 1714 and 1721. On 1 May 1721 land was laid out to him on Dayton's Right, next to his Chestnut Pound land, and by June 1737 he owned the westernmost fifteen-acre lot in Fireplace Neck \"at South\", where a new highway was laid along the lots at his and his neighbours' request. In October 1741 he sat as one of four arbitrators in a town dispute. Fireplace Neck lies a few miles west of Moriches, the home of James Smith and Jerusha Topping, whose daughter Temperance married James's son John in 1752. His brother John of Southold, who died in 1743, left Brookhaven land to \"my brother James Tuthill\" in his will of 1740.\n\nRated at Brookhaven in 1741 but gone by 1749, James moved between 1742 and 1748 to the highlands of Ulster and Orange counties, as did his brother Freegift Tuthill (1698–1765), who settled at New Windsor and Goshen. His son James Jr., born in 1717, died young at New Windsor in 1756, leaving five children and naming \"my father, James Tuthill, and Selah Strong, both of Orange County\" as his executors. His son Daniel of Goshen, whose will of 1761 asked for a burial \"decent without Pomp or State\", and a son Benjamin are also recorded, \"and perhaps others\"; the son John through whom John descends is not named in the older lists, which is why that link remains probable rather than certain. James died about 1772 in Orange County, New York. He was John's 8th-great-grandfather.",
