   "summary": "Lawyer, Southold founder and first High Sheriff of Yorkshire on Long Island (about 1608–1671), a member of Governor Nicolls's council; John's 10th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "William Wells was born in England about 1608; tradition makes him a native of Norwich, and his tombstone gives his age as 63 at his death on 13 November 1671. He is said to have come from England on 19 June 1635 with John Baylis. He was at Southampton on Long Island by 1643, when, as \"William Wells, Gent.\", he was censured for some unreverent speeches to Daniel Howe. He then settled at Southold, where he was the leading founder after the Rev. John Youngs and served as the town's lawyer.\n\nHe became a member of the council, a justice of the peace, and in 1664 the first High Sheriff of Yorkshire, the colonial county that took in Long Island, Staten Island and Westchester. The same year he sat on Governor Nicolls's five-member council and helped draft the Duke's Laws. His public offices make him a qualifying ancestor for the Society of Colonial Wars.\n\nAbout 1650 he married Bridget, the widow of Henry Tuthill, and became guardian of her Tuthill children. About 1654 he married Mary, whose surname is not known, the mother of his daughter Mehitable, born in 1666. His will of 13 November 1671 left everything to \"my well beloved wife Mary Wells\". She later married Thomas Mapes Jr., and in 1678 gave property she had received from Wells to her daughter Mary and son-in-law John Youngs; a 1677 record also mentions meadow she had given in part payment for teaching her son William his trade. His tombstone in the old Southold burying ground has been called possibly the oldest tombstone in Suffolk County.\n\nAn older claim that his parents were the Rev. William Wells, chaplain to the Queen of Denmark, and Anne Elizabeth Hunt of St Albans has been disproven: that clergyman's son William became vicar of North Elmham and died in England in 1680. William Wells is John's 10th-great-grandfather, and through him John is a cousin of First Lady Anna Symmes Harrison and President Benjamin Harrison.",
   "notable": "First High Sheriff of Yorkshire (Long Island), 1664; Gov. Nicolls's council; helped draft the Duke's Laws.",
