   "summary": "John's 4th-great-grandfather, a coal miner at Mardyke in the Slieveardagh coalfield of Tipperary who died in Ireland before his widow and sons emigrated.",
   "bio": "Thomas Heffernan was baptized at Gortnahoe in County Tipperary, Ireland, on 10 September 1808, the son of Humphrey Heffernan, 'a miner living at Lickfin', and Anastasia Ryan. On 3 February 1833 he married Catherine Boney at Gortnahoe, and the couple lived at Mardyke, where Thomas, like his father, worked as a coal miner. Mardyke and Lickfinn were collieries in the Slieveardagh coalfield; the Mining Company of Ireland had opened its Mardyke mine in 1824 with a Cornish engine and 25 workers' cottages.\n\nTheir children, all baptized at Gortnahoe, were Jude (14 July 1839), Andrew Humphrey (3 April 1842), Richard (22 December 1844), Catherine (6 February 1848) and John (29 August 1852). Thomas died in Ireland, probably between 1852 and about 1864; his son Andrew's obituary speaks only of 'his mother and brothers' coming to America. Thomas's brother Richard married Anastasia Boney, who may have been Catherine's sister, and his brothers James and Patrick were transported to New South Wales after the Ballynonty killing of 1834.\n\nHe is not to be confused with the Thomas Heffernan baptized in 1791, son of Patrick Heffernan and Catherine Neagle, who married Catherine Shelly in 1831; that was Thomas of Lanespark, a different man, and no proven link exists between the two families. Thomas of Mardyke was John's 4th-great-grandfather.",
