   "summary": "Tipperary miner's widow who emigrated with her children to Plymouth, Pennsylvania, in the 1860s and lived to ninety; John's 4th-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Catherine Boney was baptized at Gortnahoe in County Tipperary on 28 February 1814, the daughter of Thomas Boney, a farmer at Lickfin, and Jude, or Judith, Quigley; her sponsor was John Boney. On 3 February 1833 she married Thomas Heffernan at Gortnahoe, and the couple lived at Mardyke, near Killenaule. Their first child was named Jude after Catherine's mother. Her probable sister Anastasia Boney married Thomas's brother Richard.\n\nAfter Thomas died in Ireland, Catherine emigrated with her children to Plymouth in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, about 1864 to 1866. In 1880, aged 66, she was living there with her daughter Catherine Healy. She died at Plymouth on 18 April 1904, aged ninety, and was buried there two days later; her obituary appeared in the Wilkes-Barre Record under the heading 'Mrs. Catherine Heffernan'. The death register gave her mother's name as Ann rather than Jude, probably because the informant confused her with Anastasia 'Anty' Boney; the baptism is the better record.",
