   "summary": "Born in Baden in 1866 and brought to Pennsylvania as a toddler; an iron moulder for nearly fifty years in Hazleton, Tamaqua and Wilkes-Barre. John's 2nd great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Henry J. Meier was born on 15 November 1866 in Baden, Germany, presumably at Munzingen near Freiburg, the fourth child and second son of Friedrich Mayer, a mason, and Maria Anna Birkenmaier. He was still a toddler when the family, parents and four small children, crossed to America in 1868 and settled at Hazleton in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, where he grew up in the East Ward and was at school in 1880. The family name, written Mayer in Munzingen, appeared as Meyer and Myer in his boyhood; his own 1892 marriage licence, filled in by the county clerk, is the first record to spell it Meier, and that spelling stuck.\n\nOn 1 March 1892 at Hazleton he married Annie A. Higgins, who had been born at New Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, on 2 October 1867. He was then a moulder, the trade he followed for nearly fifty years, in a moulding shop and later at an iron works. Their sons Fred and Henry Jr. were born at Hazleton in 1892 and 1894; Mary and Madeline at Wilkes-Barre in 1897 and 1900; and William, about 1904, and Joseph John, in 1906, at Tamaqua in Schuylkill County, near Annie's birthplace, where the family lived for a few years. Annie bore seven children, six of whom were living in 1910. By then the family was back in Wilkes-Barre, renting on Mill Street, and by 1920 they were on Barney Street, with Henry Jr. living next door. Henry had taken out his first citizenship papers by 1910 and was a naturalized citizen by 1940.\n\nBy 1935 the family owned the house at 16 Barney Street, and Henry was still working as a moulder at an iron works in 1940, aged 72, with Anna and their unmarried children Mary and Joseph at home. He was there still in April 1950, aged 83, with Anna, 82, and Mary, a saleswoman in a clothing store. Some records put his death in December 1940, but that is almost certainly a one-digit error: he died at Wilkes-Barre on 20 December 1950, aged 84, recorded under the familiar form 'Harry', ten months before Anna, who died on 6 October 1951. He was buried at St. Mary's Cemetery in Hanover Township.",
