   "summary": "Stone mason from the wine village of Munzingen near Freiburg who brought his family to Hazleton, Pennsylvania, about 1868; John's 3rd-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Friedrich Mayer was born early on 2 March 1835 at Munzingen, a wine village near Freiburg in Baden, and was baptized the same day, the legitimate son of Heinrich Mayer, citizen and mason of Munzingen, and Juliana Senn; his godmother was Maria Anna, wife of the teacher Johann Schell. He was one of at least ten children. Like his father he became a mason and, before 1862, a citizen of Munzingen.\n\nHis first child came before his marriage. On 11 January 1862 Maria Anna Birkenmaier, a single day labourer and daughter of the late master joiner Servatius Birkenmaier and Agatha Heitzler, gave birth to a son, Friedrich. At the baptism two days later the young mason came before the priest and the witnesses, declared himself the father, and asked that the boy carry his surname with all the rights of an acknowledged child. The godparents were family: Joseph Wenz, a shoemaker married to Friedrich's sister Maria Anna Creszentia, and his unmarried sister Sophie, then in domestic service at Freiburg. Friedrich and Maria Anna married at Munzingen on 20 October 1862, after banns on 12 and 19 October, one of only two weddings in the village that year; the witnesses were two master shoemakers, Joseph Wenz and, as the entry seems to read, Anton Schumann.\n\nBetween late 1866 and the autumn of 1869, most probably in 1868, Friedrich and Maria Anna emigrated to Pennsylvania with their four children Friedrich, Franziska, Maria and Heinrich, perhaps sailing from Le Havre or Antwerp. By June 1870 they were in the East Ward of Hazleton, where Friedrich, not yet a citizen, worked as a stone mason; a fifth child, Clarissa, had been born there the previous September, and the next-door neighbour, Anthony Werner, was another stone mason from Baden. In 1880 the household held Friedrich and Annie, Fred, now 18 and a coal-mine laborer, Frances, Mary, Henry, Fressa, Elizabeth, Annie and Aggathe, along with Maria Anna's brother Gabriel, a gardener. The family most likely belonged to Holy Trinity German Catholic parish in Hazleton, though that is unconfirmed.\n\nIn America the family's name shifted with each clerk: Mayer in the Munzingen registers, Meyer in 1870, Myer in 1880, and finally Meier, first written on Henry J.'s marriage docket of 1892 and used consistently from 1910 onward. Friedrich died at Hazleton on 19 February 1913 and was buried there. He is John's 3rd-great-grandfather.",
