   "summary": "Munzingen-born daughter of the glazier Servatius Birkenmayer, wife of Friedrich Mayer and mother of eleven, who died at Hazleton in 1911; John's 3rd-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Maria Anna Birkenmayer was born at Munzingen in Baden on 29 March 1837 and baptized on 2 April, the eldest of the seven children of Servatius Birkenmayer, the village glazier, and Agatha Heizler; the schoolteacher Johann Schell stood witness. Her father died on 19 April 1853, when she was sixteen, and her little brother Heinrich followed him within weeks. Earlier accounts placed the family at Kirchzarten, but the records show that they belonged to Munzingen.\n\nIn January 1862 she was 'the single day labourer Maria Anna Birkenmaier', and on 11 January that year she gave birth at Munzingen to a son, Friedrich, whom the mason Friedrich Mayer acknowledged as his at the baptism two days later. She and Friedrich married at Munzingen on 20 October 1862, the entry describing her as the daughter of the late master joiner Servaz Birkenmaier. Her widowed mother Agatha died at Munzingen on 3 August 1867, aged 54, probably the last tie holding her there, and in about 1868 the Meiers left for America with their four children.\n\nThe family settled at Hazleton, Pennsylvania, where she appears as 'Anna, 33, Baden' in the 1870 census and as 'Annie, 43' in 1880, when her brother Gabriel, a gardener of 29, was living with them; Gabriel and another brother, Joseph, later settled at Sandusky, Ohio. She bore eleven children in all. She died at Hazleton on 30 December 1911 and was buried there under the name Mary Anne Berkmeier Meier. She was John's 3rd-great-grandmother, and should not be confused with the Mrs. Anna Meier of Wilkes-Barre who died in 1897.",
