   "summary": "John's 10th-great-grandfather; founder and patentee of Smithtown on Long Island, known as Bull Smith.",
   "bio": "Richard Smith, remembered as Bull Smith or the Patentee, was born in England about 1613. Of his earlier life, as one historian wrote, 'we know absolutely nothing'; some have said he came from a village in Yorkshire, but the evidence is thin. The most probable identification is with the Richard Smith who sailed from England on 2 October 1635, aged 22, in the ship John of London bound for St. Christophers, and his court-hand script suggests he was brought up in London. The Gloucestershire origin once given to him belongs to a different Richard Smith of Rhode Island, and a Bedfordshire origin has no source. The parents sometimes assigned to him, Sir Samuel Smith and Rebecca Buswell, are unsupported. Family tradition says his father used the blunderbuss 'Old Crib' in Cromwell's wars.\n\nIn April 1640, in Massachusetts Bay, 'Mr. Smith' petitioned to be released from his published intention to marry Sarah Hammond; he lost, and the marriage went ahead. His wife was Sarah Hammond, not the Sarah Folger of Boston given in older accounts. His first home lot at Southampton on Long Island, in the early 1640s, lay 'at the south end of the village, on the west side of the main street'. He probably went back to England in 1654 and returned in 1656 aboard the Speedwell with the first Quaker missionaries to New England, though this is debated; Southampton later banished him.\n\nSmithtown came to him through Lion Gardiner, who had received the land for helping to recover the kidnapped daughter of Sachem Wyandanch and sold it to Smith in 1663; a patent of 1665 confirmed Smith's claim. The story that he won the land by riding a bull around it in a day is, according to local historians, a myth. He was styled Major Richard Smith, and his deeds of 1688, made as 'Gent.' with his wife Sarah, gave his son Job fifty acres at the northeast branch. His children were Jonathan, the eldest, whose daughter Deborah married Joseph Blydenburgh; Richard 2d; Job; Daniel; Adam, born in 1649; Elizabeth, who married William Lawrence, Governor Philip Carteret and Col. Richard Townley; and Deborah, who married Maj. William Lawrence Jr. He died at Smithtown in 1692. He was John's 10th-great-grandfather.",
   "notable": "Founder and patentee of Smithtown (patent 1665).",
