   "researchNotes": "Sailed from Ipswich on the Francis in April 1634, aged 10, with her mother (47), Elizabeth (15) and John (7) (Hammond 1902). No Lavenham baptism under 'Sarah' found; Hammond's register list has 'Marie, daughter of William Hamond' dated 21 Oct '1628', probably a misprint for 1623 (it sits before the 1626 entry); WikiTree Hammond-356 suggests she was christened 'Marie' 21 Oct 1623 (speculative). Married Richard Smith in 1640 despite his attempt to withdraw. Her will of 1707/8 left 'my son Richard's eldest sonne my blunderbuss' — 'Old Crib', which family tradition says the Patentee's father used in Cromwell's wars; in 1898 it was held by the heirs of Judge J. Lawrence Smith. The will also left Mr. George Phillips a cow and made son Richard give up debts he 'pretends' were owed him (Pelletreau pp. 37–38). Pelletreau (1898) had called her 'Sarah Folger, of Boston, according to all accounts' — superseded.\n\nPelletreau prints the 1688 deeds that Sarah Smith joined as party ('by and with the consent of Sarah his wife'), acknowledged with her husband on 17 Sept 1688 ([R4-S1]). Her maiden name and Lavenham baptism were not re-examined this round."
