   "summary": "Palatine farmer from Jettenbach who crossed on the Phoenix in 1754 and settled beside the Delaware just north of Easton; John's 6th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Johann Andreas Grub was born on 12 September 1727 at Jettenbach in the Duchy of Palatinate-Zweibrücken, in the Kusel district, the son of Johann Nickel Grub and Elisabeth Margaretha Lampert, and was baptized on the 21st of the month in the Lutheran parish of Reichenbach; his godparents included Maria Catharina, wife of Peter Grub, and Maria Elisabetha, wife of Adam Wendel. The baptism register fixes the date, and the 12 December on his gravestone is presumably a misreading. He married Maria Elisabetha Christmann at Jettenbach on 19 September 1752.\n\nHe was probably the \"Anderas Groh\" who landed at Philadelphia on the ship Phoenix on 1 October 1754; his presumed brother Johann Peter Grub (1724–1802) had come on the Windsor the year before. In Pennsylvania he belonged to the Williams Township congregation in 1759, was on the Bethlehem Township tax list in 1772 and at St. John's Lutheran Church in Easton in 1773, and had settled in Forks Township by 1781, where he held 100 acres in 1788. His tract adjoined the Delaware just north of colonial Easton, near the present campus of Lafayette College. An \"Andrew Grub\" of the Northampton County militia received a Militia Loan certificate during the Revolution, but that is not proof of service and may concern another man. Nor should he be confused with an earlier Andreas Grub whose wife Catharina had daughters baptized in Williams Township between 1748 and 1751, before this Andreas arrived.\n\nHis will, made on 11 November 1791, named his wife Mary Elizabeth and his children Philip, Catherine (who married Gottlieb Hipp), Mary (who married George Stacher), Elizabeth (who married Peter Michael), John, Frederick and Andrew, with the widow and Frederick as executors. He died in Forks Township on 6 August 1792 and was buried at St. John's Lutheran Cemetery, Easton. He was John's 6th-great-grandfather.",
