   "summary": "John's 11th-great-grandfather; Bedfordshire-born captain, Wethersfield deputy, Connecticut charter patentee and Southampton magistrate.",
   "bio": "Thomas Topping was baptized as 'Thomas Toppyn' at Totternhoe in Bedfordshire, England, on 19 March 1608/9, the son of John Topping. He married Emma Aldridge at Dunstable on 16 November 1628, and their eldest son Thomas was baptized at Totternhoe on 25 October 1629. By 1636 he was at Wraysbury in Buckinghamshire, the parish that contains Magna Carta Island, where on 12 April of that year he was one of two overseers who reported to the manor court that 'all was well with the water courses'.\n\nHe was in New England by 1639, when he was Wethersfield's deputy to the Connecticut General Court. He moved to Milford in 1639 or 1640, joined the Milford church with his wife Emma in 1640, and had two sons baptized there, Elnathan on 2 August 1640 and James on 12 February 1643. He then settled at Southampton on Long Island, where he was 'many years a magistrate'. 'Topping's Purchase' was secured by Indian deeds of 1662 and 1666, paid for with twenty fathoms of wampum. In 1662 he was named in King Charles II's charter for Connecticut, 'one of the company to whom King Charles granted his letters patent for the Colony of Connecticut'.\n\nHe married three times. After Emma's death he made a marriage contract on 20 October 1666 with Mary, widow of Timothy Baldwin, and by June 1678 his third wife was Lydia, widow of John Wilford. On 5 October 1686, at Branford, he deeded his Southampton lands to his sons Elnathan and James. He died at Branford, Connecticut, in December 1687. The old account that he fled England as a 'refugee from religious persecution' is family tradition only. His service as captain, magistrate and deputy is of the kind that qualifies descendants for the Society of Colonial Wars, though John's descent through Temperance Smith depends on her parentage, which is not yet settled. He was John's 11th-great-grandfather.",
   "notable": "Patentee in the 1662 Connecticut charter.",
