   "summary": "Eldest brother of Henry J. Meier, born at Munzingen in 1862 and a coal-mine laborer at Hazleton; John's 3rd-great-uncle.",
   "bio": "Frederick Meier was born at Munzingen in Baden on 11 January 1862, nine months before his parents Friedrich Mayer and Maria Anna Birkenmaier married; he was baptized under his mother's name, and the entry records his father's formal acknowledgement of him. He gave the same birth date himself when he married, so the date of 11 November 1862 sometimes attached to him is a misreading. He was the eldest brother of Henry J. Meier and John's 3rd-great-uncle.\n\nHe came to America with his family as a small boy and appears as an eight-year-old born in Baden in the 1870 census at Hazleton, Pennsylvania. By eighteen he was working as a coal-mine laborer. On 12 May 1886 he married Anna Wenner at Hazleton. He may have been the 'Fred. Meier' who served as a Democratic conferee in 1893. He died at Hazleton on 15 March 1915, very probably the 'Fred Meires' of the state death index for that year, and was buried there. When his daughter Agnes, a clerk of 27, married Patrick Sweeney in February 1918, she recorded her father as deceased and born in Germany, and her mother as Anna Wenner of Hazleton.",
