   "summary": "Scranton-born colliery laborer and railroad car repairman, son of Guardia Lombardi immigrants, husband of Mollie Genett; John's maternal great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "James Petriello was born on 6 December 1910 at Scranton, Lackawanna County, Pennsylvania, the son of John Petriello, a merchant, and Maria Antonia DiBiasi, both immigrants from Guardia Lombardi in Italy. He grew up in the family's house at 1028 Bunker Hill Street in Scranton's 10th Ward, in the Guardiese neighbourhood that straddled the Scranton line and Dunmore, which is why the family appears in Scranton records but married and was buried in Dunmore. Like most of his brothers and sisters he generally used the spelling Petrillo. He was not the Chicago musicians' union leader James C. Petrillo (1892–1984).\n\nIn June 1931, aged 20 and working as a laborer, he applied to marry Mollie Genett, also 20, of Sand Street in Dunmore; because both were minors, his father John Petrillo and her mother Angelina Genett gave their consent. They were married at Dunmore on 12 July 1931 by the Rev. Michael DeLarno.\n\nIn April 1940 James, who had finished the seventh grade, was a colliery laborer renting one of the units in his late father's house on Bunker Hill Street, with his brothers Nicholas and Charles and their wives in the others. When he registered for the draft that October he was working for the Penn Anthracite Coal Co. on Nay Aug Avenue, and was described as 5 ft 3 in tall and 148 lb, with brown eyes, brown hair and a dark complexion. By 1950 he had moved to Sand Street in Dunmore, the street Mollie had grown up on, and was a railroad car repairman, the same job as his brother-in-law Anthony Genett a few doors away, while Mollie worked part-time as a sewing-machine operator in a lingerie factory. He died at Scranton on 15 December 1998 and was buried at Dunmore. He was John's maternal great-grandfather.",
