   "summary": "Guardia Lombardi-born grocer of Scranton's Bunker Hill, one of the first of its Guardiese colony, and father of James Petriello; John's maternal 2nd-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "John Petriello was born Giovanni Petriello at Guardia Lombardi, in the province of Avellino, at three in the afternoon of 25 October 1869, the son of Pietro Petriello, a thirty-three-year-old farm labourer and son of the late Angelo, and Grazia di Marino, daughter of Gaetano. He emigrated to the United States as a young man; the records give his arrival variously as 1880, 1890 and 1892, and he may well have crossed more than once. He became a naturalized citizen in 1900. He was John's 2nd-great-grandfather on his mother's side.\n\nHe married Maria Antonia DiBiasi, known as Marie or Mary, and by 1897 he was keeping a grocery store on Bunker Hill, on the Scranton and Dunmore border, which made the couple among the earliest settlers from Guardia Lombardi there, before the forty or so Guardiese families founded St. Rocco's Church in 1905. Their son Charles was born at Pittston in 1906, so the family lived briefly in Luzerne County. In 1910 John was running his own grocery on Ash Street and owned his house with a mortgage; by 1920 he was a merchant at 1028 Bunker Hill, owning the house free of debt. His children included Katie, Peter, Frank, Joseph, Anthony, Charles, Ralph, James, Michael, Rocco, Carrie, Josephine and Nicholas, most of whom used the spelling Petrillo; Peter and Frank were working as coal company and coal breaker labourers in 1920.\n\nJohn was still in the grocery business in 1934. He died at Scranton on 25 April 1939, and his wife had also died by 1941. The family kept the Bunker Hill house, where his sons James, Nicholas and Charles were each renting a unit in 1940.",
