   "summary": "Huguenot-descended New Yorker, Revolutionary militia private and prisoner of war, who lived at Cheesecocks in Orange County; John's 7th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Jean Lamoreaux, known as John, was born in New York City on 31 December 1723 and baptized at the French Church there on 12 January 1724, a son of Daniel Lamoureux and Jeanne Masse and a grandson of the Huguenot immigrant André Lamoureux. He married Charity Davenport, daughter of Capt. Thomas Davenport, who in 1752 deeded land in Goshen precinct to \"Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree\", beside her brother Oliver Davenport. A family record of 1919 remembered her as \"Charity Danforth\", which was simply the local way of saying Davenport.\n\nIn the Revolution he signed the Articles of Association, served as a private in the Orange County militia and was taken prisoner of war; the Daughters of the American Revolution recognise him as a patriot ancestor. His son John was captured at Fort Montgomery in October 1777.\n\nThe 1790 census found him at New Cornwall, Orange County, as \"John Lomereaux\", heading a household of four men aged sixteen or over, three women or girls and three enslaved people, so this branch of the family were slaveholders. Two lines above him was Peter Lomereaux, presumably his brother Pierre, who lived at Cornwall, and further down the page were Oliver Davenport and a David Prindle, of the family that later married into the Lamoreaux line.\n\nHe lived at Cheesecocks, now Monroe, and died there in 1809. The exact day is uncertain: the 28 November often given for him may really belong to his brother André, born in 1722, who died at Yorktown, Westchester County, on that date, while another record gives 24 July 1809. A claim that he was buried at Seneca Union Cemetery at Valois in Schuyler County is unlikely for a man who died at Monroe. His descendants spread to Pennsylvania, Utah and Arizona.",
