   "researchNotes": "The tree shows John Young Sr (1751-1813), with a document image, as father of Mary Young. 'Young' is too common in Northampton County to resolve online, and both he and his wife carry identical 1751-1813 dates. Lead (speculative): a 'Johannes Young/Jung' heads both lists of the 1754 Phoenix, the same ship as Andreas Grub; if he came from the Kusel area he could be the father of a John b. 1751 (then a 3-year-old, not listed by name). The Jettenbach Grubs had married Jungs for generations. Plumb's 1885 History of Hanover's John Young is a 1770s Paxtang/Lancaster 'Stewart associate', a different man. No SAR record for a John Young of Northampton was found.\n\nThe FamilySearch tree has an independent profile for Mary (Young) Croup's father: 'Johannes Jung', of Easton, born about 1745 and dead before 12 September 1787, husband of Anna Maria Doll (1751-1813), with ten children - Maria (Jan 1776, Mrs Frederick Grub), Sara (1777), Johannes (1781-1813), Susannah (1783), Peter (1784-1854), Catherine, Elizabeth, Henry, Jacob and Margaretha ([R4-S1]). Its only cited source is the will of 'Maria Young' in Northampton County Will Book 4, p.458 (presumably his widow's, 1813). If correct, Johnny's tree has given John Young his widow's death year: the tree's 1751-1813 dates for both spouses look like a copy, and the '12 Sep 1787' terminus suggests an administration or Orphans' Court date in Northampton County."
