   "summary": "Palatine immigrant of 1739 from Ulmet who farmed in Plainfield Township, Pennsylvania; John's 7th-great-grandfather if the Doll line holds, and not a Revolutionary captain.",
   "bio": "Johann Casper Doll was baptized on 1 February 1722 in the Lutheran parish of Ulmet in the Kusel district of the Palatinate, a son of Christoffel Doll (1671-1739) and Anna Sara Schuch; the family came from the village of Erzweiler. His godparents included a Schuch, from his mother's family, and the wife of Christian Drum of Ulmet. His gravestone gives his birth as 2 February 1724, but the parish register is the better authority.\n\nIn 1739, aged eighteen, he sailed for Pennsylvania on the ship Samuel, qualifying at Philadelphia on 27 August and signing the list with a mark. With him were Stoffel Doll, 40, Philip Doll, 27, 'Christ. Shook', 48, of his mother's Schuch family, and Simon Drum, a party from Ulmet. He married Anna Margaretha Dietz, and their daughter Anna Catharine was baptized in 1757 at Tohickon Reformed Church in Bucks County. He farmed in Plainfield Township, Northampton County, where he died on 4 February 1793, and was buried at Plainfield Cemetery near Wind Gap.\n\nFamily tradition has made him a captain in the Revolution, but he is not known to have held that rank; he was about 52 in 1776. The monument on his grave, erected in 1934, honours his nephew Capt. Casper Dull (1748-1829), who did serve as a captain and is buried at McVeytown, Mifflin County, and some records have run the two men together. If the Doll connection holds, Johann Casper Doll was John's 7th-great-grandfather.",
