   "bio": "Leopoldo Ferlaino was baptized at the parish of San Tommaso d'Aquino in San Mango d'Aquino, Calabria, on 25 July 1871, the son of Fortunato Ferlaino and Rosa Moraca; his godparents were Nicola Perri and his wife Rosa Berardelli. His parents had lost an earlier son of the same name, baptized in February 1870, who died at five months old that July. His other brothers and sisters were Gennaro (1855), Raffaela (1858), Marianna (1865) and Giuseppe Antonio (1868). The Ferlainos were long established in San Mango: the parish's rolls of souls from 1674 to 1804 hold 229 entries for the name, among them Francesco Ferlaino and Fragostina Colosimo with their children Carmine, Giuseppe Antonio and others between 1771 and 1788, and the family was still numerous in the town in the 1990s.\n\nLeopoldo married Giuseppina Fiorillo (1871–1943), perhaps civilly only or in another parish, since the marriage does not appear among San Mango's church marriages. Their daughter Maria Filomena, later known as Helen, was born at San Mango on 8 October 1894, the only child of theirs baptized there. By about 1901 he had settled in Scranton, Pennsylvania, where his children Frank (about 1902–03) and Raffaela (about 1904–05) were born; the family home was on Diamond Avenue in the Providence section, where his widow still headed the household in 1920. In America the name was sometimes written Farino.\n\nHe died on 2 November 1915 at Dickson City, a borough adjoining North Scranton. His daughter Helen apparently named her fifth son, Leopold Salvatore, after him.",
