   "summary": "Kingston, Pennsylvania, farmer and father of the nurse Catherine Scott Pringle; John's 4th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Stephen J. Scott was a farmer at Kingston in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania. The records disagree about his age and origin. The 1850 census of Kingston Township lists him as a 44-year-old farmer born in Pennsylvania, with his wife Julia Ann, 41, also born in Pennsylvania, and children Henry, 21, Catharine, 14, Hannah, 10, Caroline, 9, and Ann, 4; the 1880 census likewise gives Catherine's parents as Pennsylvania-born. A county history of 1893, however, says that Stephen and Julia Ann were both born in Connecticut, married there and moved to Kingston about 1818, and Catherine herself told the 1910 census that both her parents were born in Connecticut. The likeliest reading is that Stephen was born in Pennsylvania about 1806 and that the family's Connecticut roots belong to the generation before him, which would fit the Susquehanna Company settlers of the Wyoming Valley. His Kingston neighbours in 1850 included Zeba Hoyt, a Connecticut-born farmer of 61, German miners named Shoup, Steinhower and Beck, and Welsh miners and a machinist named Davis.\n\nHis parentage is not proven. Family trees name his parents as Daniel Scott and Hannah Devens, and there is some circumstantial support: Hannah Devens, a daughter of Leonard Devens, was baptized at the Walpack Dutch Reformed Church in Sussex County, New Jersey, in 1787; a Leonard Devens of Plymouth Township left a will registered in 1839, and Elizabeth Devens, 'wife of Leonard', died on 30 January 1858 aged 80 and was buried in the Shupp Cemetery at Larksville, so a Devens family was living in Plymouth Township in Stephen's lifetime. In 1810 a 'Daniel Scott Jr.' headed a household in Pittston Township that fits a couple with a son born in 1806 and a wife born about 1787, though the census names no one but the head. A speculative lead is that a Stephen Scott was one of 14 workmen who on 30 October 1818 refused to keep working on the Wilkes-Barre to Kingston bridge unless they were paid 'in good chartered notes', described as 'the first strike which the records of Wyoming disclose'; a Stephen Scott in Falls Township in 1840 was probably a different man.\n\nStephen died at Kingston on 19 April 1856, though the 1893 county history says 1855, and was buried in Forty Fort Cemetery. By July 1860 Julia Ann was a widow living in Plymouth Township with her youngest daughter Ann. He is John's 4th-great-grandfather, through his daughter Catherine Scott Pringle, the nurse.",
