   "summary": "Wife of Stephen Scott of Kingston, widowed in 1856 and in service at Plymouth; John's 4th-great-grandmother, probably born a Watson.",
   "bio": "Julia Ann Watson was born about 1809, most likely in Pennsylvania, as she told the census takers in 1850 and 1860, though a county history of 1893 said she was born in Connecticut, and her daughter Catherine later gave Connecticut as the birthplace of both her parents. The same history made her 94 in 1893, which does not fit her recorded ages. Her maiden name of Watson rests chiefly on the gravestone index reading \"Julia A Watson Scott\", and is probable rather than proven.\n\nShe married Stephen Scott of Kingston. Widowed in 1856, she was living in Plymouth Township in July 1860, aged 50, working as a domestic, with her youngest daughter Ann. She died in Luzerne County in October 1893 and was buried in Forty Fort Cemetery on 10 October.\n\nHer parentage is unsettled. One candidate is George Watson, born in England in 1778, who lived in Huntington Township in 1810 and Dallas Township in 1820 and died in 1830; compiled trees have confused him with a Rhode Island family, and the other children they give him include Jairus Harrison Watson, who married a Rebecca Van Loon, and Sabina (Watson) Partington of Plymouth Township. Another is Amariah, or Amaziah, Watson, a Plymouth taxable in 1796 alongside Michael Scott, Samuel Pringle and the Lamoreuxs, who married the widow of Noah Buck, a Connecticut settler killed in the 1778 battle, and who \"settled just below the Scott house, on the Huntington creek road\", the old Scott house built by Obadiah Scott. That put a Watson family next door to a Scott family in Huntington Township, where the Pringles were later buried in the Scott Cemetery at Huntington Mills. Neither man is proven to be her father.",
