   "summary": "Widowed head of the Ferlaino boarding house on Diamond Avenue, Scranton, that took in newcomers from San Mango d'Aquino; John's maternal 2nd-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Giuseppina Fiorillo was baptized at San Mango d'Aquino in Calabria on 11 January 1871 as Josephina, the first child of Domenico Gabriele Fiorillo and Saveria Colosimo, who had married in the parish on 1 April 1870; her godfather was Felice Sacco. Gabriele Fiorillo was the family's double surname at the time. Her younger brothers and sisters were Carmelo, Filomena, Carolina, Carmine, Antonio and Gaetano, born between 1873 and 1886. She married Leopoldo Ferlaino, and she was John's maternal 2nd-great-grandmother.\n\nShe came to America in 1900, or 1901 by another account, with her daughter Helen. By 1920 she was a widow and the head of her own household at 1306 Diamond Avenue in Scranton, Pennsylvania, a house she owned free of mortgage. Living with her were her daughter Helen, her son Frank, her daughter Rofella, her son-in-law Frank Cognetti, three grandsons and two boarders, Anthony Epifano and Angelo Bonacci; the Ferlaino house on Diamond Avenue was remembered as the place that took in boarders newly arrived from San Mango d'Aquino in the 1910s and 1920s. The census that year gave her age as 45, about four years younger than she really was. She died in Scranton on 4 June 1943, recorded under the American spelling Josephine Farino.",
