   "bio": "Fred Aloysius Meier was born at Hazleton, Pennsylvania, on 15 September 1892, the eldest brother of John's great-grandfather William F. Meier. By 1910, aged seventeen, he was a labourer in a moulding shop. On 21 April 1915, in Luzerne County, he married Anna Mather, who was born on 28 August 1892. When he registered for the First World War draft in June 1917, under the name Frederick Aloysius Meier, he was living at his parents' home on Barney Street in Wilkes-Barre and working as a moulder at the Vulcan Iron Works, with a wife and one young child to support. The couple's children were Florence Irene (1916–1933), Fred A. Jr. (1918–2000), Gerald Francis (1921–1994) and Henry James 'Harry' (1923–1943).\n\nAnna died at Wilkes-Barre on 9 February 1940, aged 47. Two months later the census found Fred, widowed, lodging in Washington, DC, and working forty hours a week as a moulder at the Navy Yard; he had still been living in Wilkes-Barre in 1935. His youngest son Harry enlisted in Washington in 1942 and was killed in Italy in July 1943 as a staff sergeant in the Army Air Forces. By 1942 Fred had married again, to Lucy Virginia (Puterbaugh) Bartleson (1878–1959), a widow fourteen years his senior. In 1957 he was living at Dunellen, New Jersey.\n\nHe died in Bristol Township, Bucks County, on 6 March 1966, aged 73, though some family records give 10 March at Trevose, and was buried in St. Mary's Cemetery, Hanover Township.",
