   "summary": "Daughter of Capt. Thomas Davenport of Philipstown, wife of John Lamoree and mother of Ensign Thomas Lamoreaux; John's 7th-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Charity Davenport was a daughter of Capt. Thomas Davenport (1682-1759) of Philipstown, in what is now Putnam County, New York, by his second wife Elizabeth, who is believed to have been a Leggett of Fordham. Her father's will, made in 1746, names her among 'my Children, Oliver, Charity, Phebe, Robert, Hannah, Stephen, and Abigail Davenport', the younger family who were still to be brought up by his wife Elizabeth and paid their shares as they came of age, which shows that Charity was then an unmarried minor. By 29 July 1752, when her father deeded her land in Goshen precinct, Orange County, next to her brother Oliver's, she was 'Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree'. She therefore married between 1746 and 1752, though the family genealogy also says she married in 1745 and that her son Thomas was born that year, and the two statements cannot both be right.\n\nShe was the mother of Ensign Thomas Lamoreaux and John's 7th-great-grandmother. A family record printed in 1919 calls her 'Charity Danforth', but 'Danforth's' was simply how people in the Hudson Highlands said 'Davenport's'; the same form appears on Erskine's Revolutionary map of West Point.",
