   "researchNotes": "Charity Davenport was a daughter of Capt. Thomas Davenport (1682-1759) of Philipstown; which of his wives was her mother is unchecked (the second wife Elizabeth was 'believed' to be a Leggett). In 1752 her father deeded Goshen-precinct land to 'Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree', next to her brother Oliver Davenport. The 1919 Lamoureux Record calls her 'Charity Danforth' - 'Danforth's' was simply how people in the Hudson Highlands said 'Davenport's'; it even appears on Erskine's Revolutionary map of West Point.\n\nThe text of her father's will, printed in the Davenport genealogy, settles her mother. The will names the first family (Thomas, William, and the children of his daughters Sarah Mandeville, Mary Henion and Elizabeth Nelson) separately from 'my Children, Oliver, Charity, Phebe, Robert, Hannah, Stephen, and Abigail Davenport'. Those seven were still to be brought up by his wife Elizabeth and paid their shares as they came of age ([R4-S1]). Charity was therefore a daughter of the second wife, Elizabeth. The will also shows she was an unmarried minor (under 18) when it was made in 1746. She was 'Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree' by the July 1752 deed, so she married between 1746 and 1752. That conflicts with the book's own statement that she married in 1745 and that her son Thomas was born in 1745 ([R4-S1]). The second wife's maiden name Leggett is only 'believed' in the book: she is supposed to be a sister of Sarah, Martha and Alice Leggett of Fordham, and the first wife is also only 'believed' to be Sarah Leggett ([R4-S1])."
