   "summary": "Pioneer of Philipstown in the Hudson Highlands, called \"Captain\", and commissioner of highways for Dutchess County; John's 8th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Thomas Davenport was born in 1682, according to the Davenport family Bible, and was probably a son of William Davenport senior of the Manor of Fordham, who conveyed land \"to my son Thomas Davenport\"; a 1718 deed names William Jr. and Thomas as \"sons of William Davenport\". A family tradition of descent from the Rev. John Davenport of New Haven has been shown to be untrue, and a link to the Davenports of Bromhall (Bramhall) in Cheshire is unsupported.\n\nHe became a pioneer of Philipstown in the Hudson Highlands, in what was then Dutchess and is now Putnam County, New York, where he farmed and served as commissioner of highways for Dutchess County from 1745 to 1749. He was known as \"Captain\", though the title is recorded only alongside his service as highway commissioner and may have been a courtesy or militia title; no militia commission for him is known, which matters for any Society of Colonial Wars claim based on him. In 1752 he deeded land in Goshen precinct to his daughter \"Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree\", the line through which he is John's 8th-great-grandfather.\n\nHe made his will on 10 October 1746 while \"sick and weak in body\", but recovered and lived until at least 25 August 1759, when, according to the family Bible, he presented that Bible to his grandson Thomas, born 11 April 1750. The will divides his family in two: on one side his sons Thomas and William and the children of his married daughters Sarah Mandeville, Mary Henion and Elizabeth Nelson, and on the other seven minor children, Oliver, Charity, Phebe, Robert, Hannah, Stephen and Abigail, left in the care of his second wife Elizabeth, who was believed to be a sister of the Leggett family. He owned at least one enslaved person, left to his wife for bringing up the children. He died on 30 December 1759 at Philipstown, and his will was proved in 1760.",
