   "researchNotes": "Thomas Davenport was born in 1682 (Davenport family Bible) and died in 1759; his will was dated 1746 and proved 1760. He was a pioneer of Philipstown in the Hudson Highlands, commissioner of highways for Dutchess County 1745-49, and called 'Captain'. In 1752 he deeded Goshen-precinct land to his daughter 'Charity Lamoree, wife of John Lamoree'. Westchester deeds show William Davenport senior of the Manor of Fordham 'conveyed to my son Thomas Davenport' land, and a 1718 deed names William Jr. and Thomas as 'sons of William Davenport' (probable father). His second wife Elizabeth was 'believed' to be a Leggett sister (WikiTree's chain Leggett -> Richardson -> Mead of Watford rests on that belief; speculative). Family descent from Rev. John Davenport of New Haven is disproven ('the last, we know, to be untrue', Giles & Franklin p.xix), and the Davenport of Bromhall/Bramhall, Cheshire link is unsupported. Possible Society of Colonial Wars qualifying ancestor.\n\nThe full text of his will, dated 10 Oct 1746 while he was 'sick and weak in body', is printed in the Davenport genealogy ([R4-S1]). He recovered and lived until at least 25 Aug 1759, when, according to the family Bible, he presented that Bible to his grandson Thomas (born 11 Apr 1750). The will divides his family in two. The first family is sons Thomas and William, plus the children of his married daughters Sarah Mandeville, Mary Henion and Elizabeth Nelson. The second family is seven minor children, Oliver, Charity, Phebe, Robert, Hannah, Stephen and Abigail, left in the care of his wife Elizabeth. He owned at least one enslaved person, left to his wife for bringing up the children ([R4-S1]). The book gives his rank of captain only from the highway-commissioner record of 1745-49, so 'Captain' may be a courtesy or militia title. No militia commission was found, which matters for any Society of Colonial Wars claim. The genealogy gives his death date as 30 Dec 1759 ([R4-S1])."
