   "summary": "Huguenot shipmaster and Gironde pilot from Meschers in Saintonge who fled to Bristol in 1689 and reached New York by 1700; John's 9th-great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "André Lamoureux was born on 3 November 1663 and baptized on 25 November at the Reformed Church of Cozes, the Protestant parish about eight kilometres inland from Meschers in Saintonge, the son, in all probability, of Daniel Lamoureux and Marie Touchay. He became a maistre de navire, a shipmaster, and a licensed pilot of the dangerous mouth of the Gironde at Meschers-sur-Gironde. He was not from La Rochelle, as has sometimes been said; that confusion probably arose from a Mersereau in-law, Suzanne Doucinet, who did come from that city.\n\nAs a Protestant he fled France with his family in 1689 and settled in Bristol, England. A daughter Judith was baptized there on 16 December 1689; a son Jacques, who had come with them from France, was buried on 19 March 1690; a Daniel born in 1693 died young; and a second Daniel, John's ancestor, was born on 29 November 1695. André was granted denization on 22 June 1694. By 15 May 1700 he was in New York, where his wife, probably Suzanne de la Tour, who kept her maiden name in the French manner, stood godmother at the French Church that month, and where he signed the register as Andre Lamourau at a wedding on 13 January 1703. The 1703 census lists him as Andrew Lamarue in the West Ward. On 4 May 1706 Lord Cornbury ordered \"Andrew L'Amoureux, captain of a merchantman\" released from impressment into the Royal Navy.\n\nAfter that order he disappears from the records; the date of his death is unknown, and Suzanne was last seen in 1720. He is John's 9th-great-grandfather.",
