   "summary": "West Pointer, Army pilot and Pershing's 'chief air scout' in 1916, who set a 250-mph speed record in 1923 and commanded France Field, Panama; John's 2nd-great-grand-uncle.",
   "bio": "Leo Gerald Heffernan was born on 23 April 1889. He graduated from West Point in 1911 and was commissioned in the 5th Cavalry, serving at Fort Myer, Virginia, in 1913 and 1915. In May 1916 he earned pilot certificate no. 489 and flew with the 1st Aero Squadron on the Mexican border and in Mexico during the Punitive Expedition. His father's obituary called him 'chief air scout to General Pershing', and his own described him as Pershing's aviation aide and pilot and 'one of the first American fliers to come under enemy gunfire'. He married Mathilde Payne in Chicago on 24 December 1915; their daughter Regina died in 1920.\n\nIn 1917, as a captain in the 3d Aero Squadron, he went to the Aviation School at San Antonio, his wife joining him there, and from November 1917 to January 1918 he commanded Wilbur Wright Field in Ohio, where a Bain News Service photograph of 1917 shows him with three other officers. He also commanded Kelly Field and led the 12th Aero Squadron. From September 1918 to May 1919, with the American Expeditionary Forces in France, he was Air Service commander of the IX Army Corps and in turn commanded the 2d Army Observation Group, the 4th and 5th Pursuit Groups and the 2d Day Bombardment Group.\n\nAfter the war he commanded the 3rd (Attack) Group at Kelly Field in 1922 and then the flying group at Fort Bliss under the 1st Cavalry Division. In February 1921 he directed the air search for Lt. Alexander Pearson, lost over the Big Bend, and in December 1922 he led five planes of the 12th Observation Squadron from Fort Bliss to join the search for Col. Francis Marshall. On 24 March 1923 the War Department announced 'the fastest flight on record': 250 miles an hour over the 75 miles from Columbus, New Mexico, to Fort Bliss at El Paso, covered in eighteen minutes in a DH-4B 'in a blinding sandstorm' with a 100-mph wind behind him, on a trip that had begun at Nogales. The record was unofficial, but Time magazine reported it and the Journal of Education ran the story as 'Outspeeding the Birds'. In April 1925 he published 'Co-operation Between Cavalry and Air Service' in The Cavalry Journal.\n\nFrom 1928 to 1930 he was assistant commandant and then commandant of the Air Corps Technical School at Chanute Field, Rantoul, and from 1930 to 1932 he commanded France Field in the Panama Canal Zone. His obituary says that 'such promising young flyers as Jimmy Doolittle and Claire Chennault' served under him, a family-supplied claim that is probably true. He was treated at Walter Reed in 1933 and retired that September as a major. From 1934 to 1949 he was the Anheuser-Busch representative in central California. His second wife was Jane Hamilton Davis (1902–1975), born at Pleasant Hill, Louisiana, and later buried at Arlington; their daughter Elizabeth Jane was born in 1922. He died of a heart attack in Los Angeles in February 1956, on the 7th or the 9th according to differing notices, and was buried at Wilkes-Barre after a funeral there on 15 February 1956.",
   "notable": "Set a 250-mph speed record on 24 Mar 1923; commanded France Field, Panama; Doolittle and Chennault served under him (obituary)",
