   "researchNotes": "Alexander J. Pringle (1824-1881), of the Kingston branch, was a trustee and treasurer of Wyoming Seminary, a founder of a Wilkes-Barre savings bank, and an organizer in 1856 of the Luzerne County party opposing the extension of slavery into free territories.\n\nAlexander Jameson Pringle was the youngest son of Thomas Pringle (d. Kingston 22 May 1861) and Elizabeth Harvey (d. Kingston 26 May 1868). He was born in Plymouth on 3 Sep 1824 and moved with the family to Kingston in 1837. He worked on his father's farm until he was past 21, then studied at Wyoming Seminary. He taught school in Kingston, and from about 1853 to 1862 he ran the principal public school in Wilkes-Barre. After 1862 he was a merchant and acted as executor, administrator or trustee of several large estates. He was a trustee of Wyoming Seminary from 1860 and its treasurer from 1872, led the Kingston M.E. choir, and helped found the Wilkes-Barre Deposit and Savings Bank in 1871, serving as its secretary. He married Mary Price at Kingston on 21 Oct 1858. Their only child was Nelson George Pringle (H0143). He had a stroke in church on 24 Jul 1881 and died on 12 Aug 1881 ([R4-S1]). His siblings were Sarah (Mrs. Elias Culver), Rosanna Harvey, Martha (d. infant), Samuel (twin of Martha; life director of Wyoming Seminary), Millicent, and the twins George Lane (H0144) and Caroline."
