   "summary": "Plymouth farmer and husband of Phoebe Davenport, father of nine; possibly John's 4th-great-grand-uncle or a cousin.",
   "bio": "John Pringle was born in Pennsylvania about 1801, a native of Plymouth in Luzerne County according to the county history. He married Phoebe Davenport (1799 to after 1873), a daughter of Thomas and Charity (Lamoreaux) Davenport, and the couple had nine children between 1821 and 1836: Thomas, Elizabeth, Clara, Lydia, William, Dennison, Charity D., who married the liveryman Andrew R. Worthington in 1855, Stephen, who died in 1850, and Martha. A county historian writing in 1873 called Jacob Gould and John Pringle, the two sons-in-law of old Thomas Davenport, \"both highly respectable men\".\n\nIn September 1850 he was a farmer in Plymouth Township, aged 49, with Phebe, who held the family's small real estate in her own name, and eight of their children at home, the eldest sons working as laborer, clerk and farmer. By 1860 Phebe was keeping house with her daughters Elizabeth and Lydia and John was gone, so he died between 1850 and 1860, probably in Plymouth Township. He was most likely a son of Samuel Pringle and Martha Lamoreaux, since Samuel and his brother James had the only Pringle families in Plymouth at the time and James is recorded with daughters only, but this has not been proven; he was therefore either John's 4th-great-grand-uncle or a cousin.",
