   "researchNotes": "John Pringle married Phoebe Davenport (1799 - after 1873), daughter of Thomas and Charity (Lamoreaux) Davenport; nine children 1821-36: Thomas, Elizabeth, Clara, Liddie, William, Dennison, Charity D. (m. liveryman Andrew R. Worthington 1855), Stephen, Martha. Phebe Pringle's will: Plymouth, 1867, vol. D p.344. Whether John was Samuel's son or a nephew is speculative.\n\nThe 1850 census of Plymouth Township (enumerated 18 Sep 1850 by A. Atherton, p.155, dwelling 1054, family 1076) shows John Pringle, 49, farmer, born Pennsylvania, with Phebe, 52 (real estate $150, in her name), and children Thomas 29 (laborer), Clarissa 27, Elizabeth 25, Lydia 24, William 22 (clerk), Denison 19 (farmer), Charity 17 and Martha 13 ([R4-S1]). Stephen (b. 1834) is absent; the FamilySearch tree says he died in 1850. This gives John a birth year of about 1801, in Pennsylvania, which fits Bradsby's statement that John and Phoebe were 'natives of Plymouth' ([R4-S3]).\n\nBy the 1860 census (Plymouth, post office Muhlenburg) Phebe Pringle, 62, keeps house with daughters Elizabeth 36 and Lydia 34, and John is gone, so he died between 1850 and 1860 ([R4-S2]). Wright (1873) calls Jacob Gould and John Pringle, the two sons-in-law of old Thomas Davenport, 'both highly respectable men' ([R4-S4]). The FamilySearch published tree places John (LZVN-JBK, b. 1801 Plymouth) as a son of Samuel Pringle and Martha Lamoreaux ([R4-S5]). A man born in Plymouth about 1801 would almost have to be a son of Samuel or of Samuel's brother James, the only Pringle families there. Giles & Franklin give James and Phebe (Lamoreux) Pringle only three daughters, and Samuel had five sons ([R4-S6]), so a son of Samuel is the better fit. This is still not proven."
