   "summary": "Brother of Samuel Pringle and husband of Phebe Lamoreux, who settled in what became Jackson Township, Luzerne County, about 1797; probably John's 5th-great-grand-uncle.",
   "bio": "James Pringle, whose name was also written Prendle and Prindle, was a brother of Samuel Pringle; the brothers were 'presumably natives of Connecticut' who lived in Orange County, New York, for several years before 1790. He is probably John's 5th-great-grand-uncle, and through his marriage he was also uncle of Martha Lamoreaux. He was not the James Pringle born about 1799 who is John's ancestor.\n\nOn 19 May 1785 Rev. Silas Constant married 'James Prendle and Phebe Lamoreux' at Smith's Clove in Orange County. Phebe (1765–1856) was a daughter of John and Charity (Davenport) Lamoreux and a sister of Ensign Thomas Lamoreaux. About 1797 or 1798 James settled in the part of Plymouth, Pennsylvania, that later became Jackson Township. The couple had three children: Charity (31 January 1800 – 1899), who married Oliver Mekeel; Hannah; and Phebe, whose daughters were Elmira and Martha, later Mrs. Clinton Brown. In her widowhood Phebe lived with her sister Charity in Plymouth and afterwards with the Mekeels.",
