   "summary": "Early Plymouth coal operator whose losses in the coal trade were blamed for his early death; John's 1st cousin 7 times removed.",
   "bio": "Daniel Davenport was born at Plymouth in Luzerne County on 7 or 9 March 1796 or 1797, the family records disagreeing on the day and year, and was John's 1st cousin seven times removed. On 12 March 1820 he married Mary Nesbitt, who had been born at Plymouth on 3 March 1803. In 1823 he and John Lamoreaux built a sawmill on Bidlack's Creek in Jackson Township.\n\nDaniel became an early coal operator, but he \"became seriously involved in the coal trade\" and lost most of his estate. Hendrick B. Wright, who had sat on the same school bench, wrote that these troubles \"undoubtedly\" caused his early death on 3 September 1840. His children were Charles (born 27 May 1821), George (1823–1906), who married Emily Ingham, Chester (1825–1855), Esther (born 1827), who married William Garrahan, Harriet (born 1830), James (born 1831) and Hendrick Wright Davenport (1834–1916), evidently named for his father's old schoolmate, who died at Culbertson, Nebraska.",
