   "researchNotes": "Judson LaMoure (1839-1918), North Dakota state senator; LaMoure County, ND, is named for him. The 1919 book says the ND LaMoures descend from James (b. 1731); exact line not traced.\n\nJudson LaMoure was born 27 Mar 1839 at Frelighsburg, Lower Canada, the youngest of five children of John Edward LaMoure (1795-1869) and Lois Louisa Perry (1805-1881); his eldest brother Edward Byron LaMoure was killed in an Indian raid at Elk Point, Dakota Territory, on 9 Aug 1865. He came to Davenport, Iowa, in 1859, tried the Pike's Peak gold rush in 1860, and from 1862 was at Elk Point. He moved to Pembina County in 1870, kept a store at Neche, and sat in the territorial and then state legislature until 1913. He married Minnie Ella Nelson on 3 Dec 1874 (six children, four surviving), and died at Stuart, Florida, on 16 Mar 1918 ([R4-S1]).\n\nThe 1919 Lamoureux Record only says the author 'thinks' the North Dakota LaMoures descend from Jacques/James Lamoureux (b. 1731, of Rensselaerville and Westerlo, Albany Co.), and that some of James's grandchildren adopted the spelling LaMoure ([R4-S2]). Judson's Quebec birth is a caution: the same publication notes separate French-Canadian Lamoureux families (from Boucherville, 1660s) in Quebec, including at Bedford, near Frelighsburg ([R4-S2]). Frelighsburg was also settled by Loyalists and New Englanders, so either origin is possible."
