   "researchNotes": "Silas W. Lamoreux (1843-1909), born Lenox, Madison Co., NY; Commissioner of the General Land Office 1893-97. The 1919 book says his family came from an Orange Co. Lamoreaux family; line not traced. Others named in the 1919 book without traced descent: Rev. Rufus Lamoreaux, Andrew (Colorado legislator), Prof. Oscar F. Lamoreaux (Wheaton), Prof. Wendell Lamoreaux (Union), Judge John J. Lamoree (Oswego).\n\nSilas Wright Lamoreux was born at Lenox, Madison Co., NY, on 8 Mar 1843 and came with his parents to Plover, Wisconsin, in 1852, joining his older brother Oliver; the family moved to Mayville a year later. He read law at Plover, was admitted at 21, served in the 5th Wisconsin Infantry in 1864-65, and returned to practise at Mayville. He sat in the Assembly in 1872, was Dodge County judge from 1877, and was Commissioner of the General Land Office under Cleveland (1893-97). At Beaver Dam he controlled the Beaver Dam Malleable Iron Works (750 employees) and was president of the German National Bank. He died at home on 5 Aug 1909 after long illness (blood poisoning, with diabetes); his funeral was held under Masonic auspices ([R4-S1], [R4-S2], [R4-S3]).\n\nThe 1919 Lamoureux Record places his family (Madison Co., NY, to Wisconsin in 1852) among the descendants of one of the Orange County Lamoreux families (John or Peter), but gives no generations ([R4-S4]). Neither obituary seen names his parents."
