   "tags": [],
   "summary": "\"An ardent patriot\" of Smithtown who signed the Articles of Association and whose house in the Village of the Branch is a local landmark; John's 1st cousin 9 times removed.",
   "bio": "Abner Smith (1734-1782), a grandson of Job Smith through Aaron Smith, was John's 1st cousin 9 times removed and the closest blood relative tied to any landmark in the Hauppauge area. Remembered as \"an ardent patriot\", he signed the Articles of Association at the Revolution. He acquired ten acres of Captain Richard Smith's 1736 tract, and his house, often called the Abner Smith House on Hauppauge Road, stood on Village Way in the Village of the Branch at Smithtown; the old Hauppauge Road ended at the Branch, so the house was never in Hauppauge itself.\n\nHis land lay on the east side of the Nissequogue River beside the Old Mill Creek, the site of Smithtown's first mill, where relics of the old dam could still be seen in 1898. In 1792 his daughter Juliana released to her brother Elisha \"all that tract of land formerly my father's Abner Smith, bounded west by the river, north and east by Wm Arthur and south by Obadiah Smith\". If Temperance Smith was a daughter of James Smith, Abner was her first cousin.",
