   "researchNotes": "Elder Joseph Blydenburgh, son of a Dutch New Yorker, married Deborah daughter of Jonathan Smith on 8 Jul 1692 (she d. before May 1699), then Catherine DeHart; founded the Blydenburgh homestead at the Branch. This younger Joseph moved to Hauppauge and survived a British search of his house for gold hidden in a churn. SAR-recognized patriot buried in Hauppauge Rural Cemetery.\n\nPelletreau's Blydenburgh family sketch ([R4-S1]) settles the open point: Joseph (1732-1833) was a son of Joseph Blydenburgh 2d and his wife (a Saxton), and Joseph 2d was a child of the immigrant's son Joseph 1st by his second wife 'Collosia' (the Catherine DeHart of other sources), not by Deborah Smith. So Joseph (1732) does not descend from Bull Smith and is not John's blood cousin through that route. His brother Daniel was killed in the French War. He married Nancy Smith, whose parentage is not stated (if she was a Bull Smith, their children would be cousins)."
