   "bio": "Selah Strong (1737–1815) was a delegate to the Provincial Congress and a captain in the Revolution, during which he was held on a British prison ship; after the war he served as a county judge. A 1792 engraving by Cornelius Tiebout shows \"the seat of the Hon. Selah Strong, Esqr., St. George's Manor\". His wife, Anna Smith Strong, is remembered as the Culper spy; she belonged to a different Smith family and is not a blood relative of John's.\n\nSelah was the son of Thomas Strong and Susannah Thompson, a granddaughter of John Thompson, which makes him John's 2nd cousin 9 times removed on the Thompson side. He was also a great-great-grandson of Bull Smith, and he is related to John more distantly through the Topping family and, if that line holds, through the Brewsters.",
