   "bio": "Abraham Woodhull was born at Setauket, New York, on 7 October 1750, the son of Richard Woodhull IV (1712–1788) and Margaret Smith (1714–1803). During the Revolutionary War he was the chief agent of Washington's Culper Ring, operating under the name 'Samuel Culper Sr.', and in later life he served as a judge. He married twice, first in the early 1780s and again on 5 October 1824. His wife Mary Smith was herself a descendant of Bull Smith through both her parents, Obadiah Smith, son of Daniel Smith 2d, and Anna, daughter of Edmund Smith 1st by his second wife Mary, so that Abraham married his half-first cousin. He died on 23 January 1826.\n\nHis kinship to John runs through his mother. Margaret Smith was a daughter of Edmund Smith 1st, the only son of Adam Smith, son of Bull Smith, and of Susannah Floyd, which makes Abraham a 3rd cousin 8 times removed of John. An older reckoning traced the connection through the Topping family, on the strength of his great-grandmother Temperance, wife of Richard Woodhull II; but she was probably Temperance Fordham, a daughter of Rev. Jonah Fordham and the widow of a Topping, so that route is doubtful.",
   "funFacts": [
    "George Washington suggested his alias 'Samuel Culper', a play on Culpeper County, Virginia."
   ],
