   "summary": "Dunmore-born daughter of Bisaccia immigrants, wife of James Petriello and mother of John T. Petriello; John's maternal great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Carmela Gianetta, known all her life as Mollie, was born at Dunmore, Pennsylvania, on 13 July 1910, the daughter of Frank Giannetta, a blacksmith, and Angelica Ferrantino, a foundling, both immigrants from Bisaccia in Italy who had married there about 1896. Her four older siblings were born in Italy; she was the first of the children born in Dunmore, arriving three months after the April 1910 census had found the family on Sand Street. Her brothers and sisters were Savino (1897-1964), Mariano Martin (1899-1941), Maria (born 1902), Antonio (1907-1998), Giuseppe (1912-1971), Rosa (born 1914) and Charles J. (1916-1975). In January 1920 she was nine years old and the only daughter at home among five brothers at 133 Sand Street in Dunmore's 5th Ward, a house that remained the family home for decades.\n\nIn June 1931, aged 20 and employed at housework, she took out a licence to marry James Petriello; because she was still a minor her mother gave consent. They were married at Dunmore on 12 July 1931 by Rev. Michael DeLarno. After the wedding she lived with James at 1028 Bunker Hill Street in Scranton, where the 1940 census recorded that she had completed the fifth grade. She was the mother of John T. Petriello, John's grandfather.\n\nBy 1950 the couple were back on Sand Street at number 128, a few doors from her widowed mother and next door to her sister Mary Pichiarello. Mollie was then working part-time as a sewing-machine operator in a lingerie factory, one of the garment shops that employed many of Dunmore's Italian women. She died at Scranton on 7 February 2000.",
