   "summary": "Landowner from Bisaccia in Campania who became a laborer and blacksmith in Dunmore, Pennsylvania; father of Mollie Gianetta and John's maternal 2nd great-grandfather.",
   "bio": "Frank Giannetta, baptized Francesco, was born on 2 May 1869 at Bisaccia, Avellino, Campania, Italy, the son of Savino Giannetta, born in 1842, and Angelamaria Fede. Bisaccia lies about twelve kilometres from Guardia Lombardi, the home of other branches of John's mother's family. On 1 August 1896 he married at Bisaccia; he was then 27 and a possidente, a man of property, and his bride, Angelica Ferrantino, was 23, a foundling born at Sant'Angelo dei Lombardi and living in Bisaccia.\n\nHe sailed from Naples on the SS Italia and landed at New York about 15 April 1903, though some records say 1906. By April 1910 he was a contractor's laborer living at 134 Sand Street in Dunmore, Lackawanna County, Pennsylvania, still an alien, with his wife, their four Italian-born children Sam, Mariano, Marie and Tony, and two Italian boarders. By January 1920 he owned the house across the street at 133 Sand Street, with a mortgage, and worked as a laborer in the railroad shops; his sons Sam and Marion worked at a coal breaker, and seven children were at home. In July 1920 he declared his intention to become a citizen at Scranton, under the name Frank Gennetti, and was naturalized there between 1920 and 1923. By 1931, when his daughter Carmela, known as Mollie, married, he was a blacksmith.\n\nHe was still at 133 Sand Street in May 1933, when his son Joseph married at St. Rocco's, the church of the Guardiese community in Dunmore. He died eleven months later, on 14 April 1934, at Dunmore. By 1940 his widow was head of the household. His name was spelled many ways in America, among them Gianetta, Genett, Gennetti, Janet and Genet. He is John's maternal 2nd great-grandfather.",
