   "summary": "Foundling of Sant'Angelo dei Lombardi, wife of Frank Giannetta and mother of Mollie, who settled in Dunmore, Pennsylvania; John's maternal 2nd-great-grandmother.",
   "bio": "Angelica Ferrantino was born about 1873 at Sant'Angelo dei Lombardi, in the province of Avellino, as a foundling: her marriage act describes her as 'figlia di padre ignoto e madre ignota', the daughter of an unknown father and an unknown mother, and the surname Ferrantino was given to her by the authorities. Sant'Angelo dei Lombardi was a district seat with a foundling wheel, and names like Ferrantino were typical of those given to abandoned infants. Her parents are unknown. Some family records give her birth instead as about 1874 at Bisaccia. Her surname was later written Ferrandino, and in America she was known as Angelina or Angelica Genett.\n\nOn 1 August 1896, at Bisaccia, where she was then living, she married Frank Giannetta; she was 23. She came to the United States in 1909 with the children born in Italy, including her son Anthony. By April 1910 she had four children, all living, and three more were born at Dunmore, Pennsylvania, where the family lived on Sand Street. When her daughter Mollie married in 1931, Angelica gave her consent to the marriage.\n\nAfter Frank's death in April 1934 she headed the household at 133 Sand Street, which she owned. In 1940 her sons Anthony and Charles J. lived with her, together with Charles's wife Jeanette and a baby granddaughter, Angela, and by 1950 Mollie's family had moved to 128 Sand Street nearby. In April 1950 she was 75, widowed and a naturalized citizen, living with Anthony and his wife Pearl. She died at Dunmore later in 1950. Her ages in successive records, from 23 at her marriage to 46 in 1920, 64 in 1940 and 75 in 1950, put her birth between about 1873 and 1875.",
